WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.650
Mary Poppins may be practically perfect in
every way, but Mary Poppins Returns has made

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.680
some interesting changes that actually contradict
some of the details and events from the original

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.680
movie.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.380
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm revealing six ways that Mary Poppins Returns

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.570
ignored what happened in the first film and
I'll also explain why.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.470
Some mild spoilers ahead though I avoid any
significant plot reveals.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.880
A snow globe of St Paul's Cathedral made a
memorable appearance in the original movie

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.729
when Mary Poppins used it in the "Feed The
Birds" lullaby she sang to young Michael and

00:00:31.729 --> 00:00:32.729
Jane.

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:36.649
And that snow globe pops up again in the sequel,
along with the wooden letter blocks and kite,

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:40.420
when the now adult Michael is searching through
the attic for something he's lost.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.649
The snow globe in this scene is used as a
nostalgic way to remind us and Michael of

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:48.890
his time with Mary Poppins, and when he comments
that he doesn't know why they've kept any

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:52.360
of this stuff, it shows us he's lost his child-like
wonder.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.249
The problem with this little easter egg though
is that it's almost impossible for Michael

00:00:56.249 --> 00:01:00.440
to still have that snow globe because, in
the original movie, Mary Poppins took it with

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.309
her when she left the Banks family.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:07.110
Yes, the magical nanny actually put the snow
globe into her carpet bag, together with all

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:11.860
her other belongings, just before her departure,
and unless she's been making secret visits

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.910
to Michael's attic to hide snow globes there
during the 20-odd years since she was last

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.180
in Cherry Tree Lane, then it shouldn't have
appeared there in the sequel!

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.510
When a robin accompanied Mary as she sang
A Spoonful of Sugar to the Banks children

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.900
in the original movie, it was a delightful
little moment.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.930
But as sweet as that song/scene is, it's also
guilty of a goof because despite the movie's

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.270
London setting, the robin that perches itself
on Mary's fingers is an American robin which

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.960
is much larger than the European robin found
in England.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.680
The sequel actually fixes the original error
via the decorative robin on Mary's hat, which

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.080
this time around is a European robin with
a much smaller red breast than its American

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.080
cousin.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.950
So, the new robin isn't just a fun easter
egg to "A Spoonful of Sugar", but also a neat

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.320
little way of correcting that movie mistake
from the first film.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.880
Now, we've gotta talk about how Emily Blunt
has introduced a Mary Poppins with a quite

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.260
distinct character to Julie Andrews' version
in the original movie.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.849
I think it's great that Blunt decided to create
her own Mary rather than try and imitate what

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:13.760
Julie Andrews did, because, as Blunt herself
has said, "No one is ever going to out-Julie

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:14.960
Julie Andrews."

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.200
I mentioned previously, in my comparison video,
how Emily Blunt looked to author P.L.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.640
Travers' Mary Poppins books for inspiration.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.850
And in those books, the magical nanny is much
more abrasive and ruder than the generally

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.260
more kind but firm character so many of us
grew to love in Julie Andrews.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.340
And that's why when Mary Poppins comes back
in the sequel, she's got a slightly weirder

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.050
edge to her and you could say she comes across
as a bit sharper.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.730
We even see a somewhat more risqué side to
Mary during her performance of the song "A

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:46.930
Cover Is Not The Book", where she drops her
new very posh pronunciation for a Cockney

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.460
accent and sings lyrics laden with adult humour.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.500
The very prim-and-proper nanny we met in the
original movie is nowhere to be found during

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.920
this vaudevillian song-and-dance number.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.709
In other words, this is not your childhood
Mary Poppins!

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:04.880
When Mary Poppins returns in the new movie,
so does her talking umbrella, but this time

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.460
around the parrot umbrella is quite different.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.930
First of all, its colour has changed from
green to a deep almost reddish brown colour.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.750
And, secondly, it's also a lot more chatty.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.680
In the original Mary Poppins film, the umbrella
was silent throughout and only spoke at the

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.090
end to complain that the Banks children weren't
grateful enough and to slightly scold Mary

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.730
for not admitting how much she really cared
about the family.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.410
"Practically perfect people never permit sentiment
to muddle their thinking."

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.880
"Is that so?

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.349
Well, I'll tell you one thing Mary Poppins,
you don't fool me a bit."

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.349
"Oh really?"

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:38.349
"Yes really.

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:40.510
I know exactly how you feel about these children."

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.020
"And if you think I'm keeping my mouth shut
any longer..."

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.780
"That will be quite enough of that, thank
you."

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:52.090
I imagine many people were pleasantly surprised
when the parrot suddenly spoke for the first

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:56.740
time at the end of the 1964 film, and I think
the creative team behind Mary Poppins Returns

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.599
realised the comic potential of giving the
parrot umbrella a bigger talking part this

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:01.599
time around.

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:05.720
So, in the new movie, the umbrella gets a
number of nice moments including some choice

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.459
comedic lines and a funny little interaction
with Dick van Dyke, which is part of the plot.

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:14.620
However, the parrot's reply that 'Grown-ups
forget, they always do', when Georgie asks

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.689
why his father doesn't believe that Mary Poppins
arrived on a kite, makes me think the new

00:04:18.689 --> 00:04:23.990
film has also given the parrot some aspects
of the talkative jackdaw in PL Travers' Mary

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:24.990
Poppins books.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.740
In the first book, for example, the jackdaw
tells two of the Banks children that as kids

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.389
grow up they forget all about the magical
things that happened.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:35.870
As for the parrot's new look, the movie's
production designer has said about the film

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.320
that "we want people to feel like they're
seeing something they've seen before, but

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.259
[they're] really seeing it for the first time."

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:47.310
Fun fact, in the 1964 movie, the parrot umbrella
was voiced by David Tomlinson, who also played

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.689
the children's father, while in Mary Poppins
Returns, the voice is provided by Edward Hibbert,

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:55.840
who's best known as the snooty restaurant
critic Gil from the TV sitcom Frasier.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.020
Next, it's time to show how the sequel's new
timeline has messed with Mary Poppins' age,

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.909
possibly confirming a popular fan theory that
Mary Poppins may well, in fact, be a time

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:06.909
lord!

00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:11.210
For anyone who doesn't know, the first Mary
Poppins movie was set in 1910 and Julie Andrews

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.759
was 28 at the time of the US release.

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:18.590
However, Mary Poppins Returns is set in the
1930s, in Depression-era London, so the events

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.990
take place around 20 to 30 years after the
first film.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.689
Emily Blunt, though, is only 35, just seven
years older than Julie Andrews was at the

00:05:26.689 --> 00:05:27.689
time.

00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:31.729
Of course, actors don't just play their own
real age, but generally have a certain range

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:34.240
within which they're able to act convincingly.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.080
However, whichever way you look at it, Emily
Blunt doesn't look anything like 48 to 58

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.289
years old, which is what Mary Poppins would
be based on Andrews' age at the time and if

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:47.449
Mary Poppins had aged 20 to 30 years like
the time-gap between the two stories.

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:52.059
All in all, it seems that Mary Poppins has
barely aged, even though the Banks children

00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:56.119
have completely grown up, as has Jack, who
knew Mary when he was a child.

00:05:56.119 --> 00:06:00.759
The sequel knows this – and to be fair – owns
the inconsistency, making a joke of it:

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:02.809
"Good heavens, it really is you!"

00:06:02.809 --> 00:06:05.059
"You seem to have hardly aged at all."

00:06:05.059 --> 00:06:06.059
"Really?!

00:06:06.059 --> 00:06:07.310
How incredibly rude!"

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:09.839
"One never discusses a woman's age Michael.

00:06:09.839 --> 00:06:11.119
Would have hope I'd taught you better."

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:15.110
As for the official explanation, the movie's
producer Marc Platt has said that "Mary's

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.379
a character who lives outside of time.

00:06:17.379 --> 00:06:18.379
She's magic.

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:21.349
And so she is the one character who actually
doesn't age."

00:06:21.349 --> 00:06:26.430
Admiral Boom and his first mate Mr Binnacle
both return for what is actually a slightly

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.099
bigger part in the sequel.

00:06:28.099 --> 00:06:32.639
Although the pair still fire their cannon
to mark the time, in Mary Poppins Returns

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:36.379
there's a running joke about how they're no
longer reliable, which is very different to

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:40.319
the reputation Boom had built up about his
timekeeping in the original movie:

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:43.199
"What he's famous for is punctuality."

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:48.979
"The whole world takes its time from Greenwich,
but Greenwich takes its time from Admiral

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:49.979
Boom."

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:54.240
When the sequel begins, we discover that Boom
and Binnacle's cannon fire now runs consistently

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.629
late when measured against Big Ben, although
Boom actually believes it's Big Ben that's

00:06:58.629 --> 00:06:59.629
wrong!

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:03.289
While this is certainly a change from the
1964 film, if you've seen the new movie, you'll

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:07.930
know that Boom's time difference with Big
Ben is part of the plot, though I won't spoil

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:08.930
the details.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:12.719
And in a little further explanation of this
change, the movie's novelisation also adds

00:07:12.719 --> 00:07:16.770
that the admiral 'had slowed down in his later
years and his sense of time had slowed with

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:17.770
him.'

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:21.870
Well, it's fun to spot these little differences,
but you should know that Mary Poppins "never

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.360
explains anything", so we can probably put
these changes down to Mary's magic.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.050
Now, what do you think were the best and worst
parts of the sequel?

